WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.075
A blast is the one planned event at a mine where

00:07.075 --> 00:10.612
the entire area has to be empty, and stay empty,

00:10.612 --> 00:13.287
until someone with authority says it's safe.

00:13.287 --> 00:15.838
This lesson is about how that works at our sites,

00:15.838 --> 00:18.625
and what your part is. For most new miners,

00:18.625 --> 00:20.863
your part is simple: know the warning,

00:20.863 --> 00:23.600
stay out, and never let anyone else in.

00:24.732 --> 00:27.595
Allen's own drill and blast crew does the drilling

00:27.595 --> 00:30.620
and blasting at Boonesboro, Clover Bottom and Dix

00:30.620 --> 00:33.532
River. At Clover Bottom, a contractor,

00:33.532 --> 00:36.582
Austin Powder, sometimes shoots on the surface.

00:36.582 --> 00:39.457
Either way, the same rules apply, and the person

00:39.457 --> 00:42.557
in charge of every shot is the blaster in charge.

00:42.557 --> 00:45.107
Licensed blasters are on each site's leadership

00:45.107 --> 00:47.694
team. You don't decide when it's safe.

00:47.694 --> 00:48.445
They do.

00:49.566 --> 00:55.828
MSHA's rule, 56 and 57 point 6306, sets the basics.

00:55.828 --> 00:58.441
Once explosives are brought to a blast site,

00:58.441 --> 01:02.316
the site is attended, or posted. Once loading begins,

01:02.316 --> 01:04.891
only blasting-related work goes on there.

01:04.891 --> 01:07.203
Before the shot, everyone has to be out of the

01:07.203 --> 01:10.341
blast area, unless they are in a proper shelter.

01:10.341 --> 01:12.791
There has to be ample warning, with a clear way

01:12.791 --> 01:14.916
out for the people who fire the shot.

01:14.916 --> 01:17.428
And every access route into the blast area has

01:17.428 --> 01:19.504
to be guarded or barricaded.

01:20.616 --> 01:22.978
Everything else in this lesson is how those words

01:22.978 --> 01:24.791
show up on our sites.

01:25.901 --> 01:28.339
A shot does more than break rock. It can throw

01:28.339 --> 01:31.539
pieces a long way, farther than most people guess,

01:31.539 --> 01:34.301
and it sends a pressure wave and a loud noise through

01:34.301 --> 01:37.151
the air. That is why the blast area is bigger than

01:37.151 --> 01:39.639
the hole pattern. The blaster in charge sets the

01:39.639 --> 01:42.326
area for each shot, and it can change from one

01:42.326 --> 01:44.939
shot to the next. So don't assume that because

01:44.939 --> 01:47.114
you were safe where you stood yesterday,

01:47.114 --> 01:49.114
you're safe in the same spot today.

01:50.256 --> 01:52.731
The best protection is knowing about the shot before

01:52.731 --> 01:55.281
it happens. At the start of every shift,

01:55.281 --> 01:57.569
you should hear whether there's a shot planned,

01:57.569 --> 02:00.444
where, and roughly when. How your site shares that

02:00.444 --> 02:03.144
is part of your site-specific orientation.

02:03.144 --> 02:06.181
If nobody has told you, ask. It's a normal question,

02:06.181 --> 02:08.832
and a good supervisor will be glad you asked.

02:12.142 --> 02:15.192
At Allen, the surface warning is a siren and strobe

02:15.192 --> 02:18.492
beacons. When you hear the siren or see the strobes,

02:18.492 --> 02:20.654
stop what you're doing and get out of the blast

02:20.654 --> 02:23.554
area, to wherever your site has told you to go.

02:23.554 --> 02:26.379
Don't finish the job first, and don't drive in.

02:27.496 --> 02:30.283
Every road into the blast area gets a guard.

02:30.283 --> 02:32.008
If you're ever assigned as a guard,

02:32.008 --> 02:34.208
and only if you're trained and assigned,

02:34.208 --> 02:36.746
you stay at your post and you stop everyone.

02:36.746 --> 02:40.058
Trucks, pickups, supervisors, everyone.

02:40.058 --> 02:42.321
You do not leave your post until the blaster in

02:42.321 --> 02:45.558
charge gives the all-clear. Your site's exact signal

02:45.558 --> 02:47.983
pattern, how long the warning runs,

02:47.983 --> 02:49.921
and where the guards stand are covered in your

02:49.921 --> 02:51.446
site-specific training.

02:52.570 --> 02:54.770
Underground, it works the same way.

02:54.770 --> 02:57.257
Once the siren goes out, every entrance to the

02:57.257 --> 02:59.833
headers is blocked, so that nobody can walk into

02:59.833 --> 03:02.170
the area where the shot is about to go.

03:02.170 --> 03:04.545
Know which headings are being shot on your shift.

03:04.545 --> 03:07.045
If you're not sure, ask your supervisor before

03:07.045 --> 03:09.458
you go anywhere near the working faces.

03:09.458 --> 03:11.820
And if you're underground when the siren sounds,

03:11.820 --> 03:14.320
stay in the place your site has told you to be,

03:14.320 --> 03:16.470
until you hear that all is clear.

03:17.596 --> 03:20.633
Before the shot, the bench itself is controlled.

03:20.633 --> 03:24.146
At our sites, access to a drill bench is bermed,

03:24.146 --> 03:26.784
and only the driller or the powder crew are allowed

03:26.784 --> 03:29.346
in. The highwall edge of the bench is marked with

03:29.346 --> 03:32.784
paint six feet back, and nobody crosses that line.

03:32.784 --> 03:35.046
If your job doesn't put you on that bench,

03:35.046 --> 03:38.209
stay off it. Radios and phones are also kept back

03:38.209 --> 03:40.584
from loaded holes, at the distance the blaster

03:40.584 --> 03:41.446
sets.

03:42.587 --> 03:45.212
The rules cover everyone on site, not just our

03:45.212 --> 03:48.449
crew. Visitors are escorted, and a good escort

03:48.449 --> 03:51.499
checks whether a shot is planned before going anywhere.

03:51.499 --> 03:53.949
Delivery drivers and contractors need to be told

03:53.949 --> 03:56.624
too. If you notice someone on site who obviously

03:56.624 --> 03:59.224
doesn't know about the blast, don't assume somebody

03:59.224 --> 04:01.612
else will tell them. Stop them politely,

04:01.612 --> 04:03.299
and let your supervisor know.

04:06.645 --> 04:09.207
Weather can change the plan. On the surface,

04:09.207 --> 04:11.782
the crew uses weather apps to check for lightning

04:11.782 --> 04:14.495
at shooting time. If lightning is present,

04:14.495 --> 04:17.707
the blast area is cleared and the entrance is guarded.

04:17.707 --> 04:20.020
The blaster in charge makes that decision.

04:20.020 --> 04:22.770
And there is a fifteen-minute wait after strikes.

04:22.770 --> 04:24.945
If a storm rolls in and you're anywhere near a

04:24.945 --> 04:27.807
loaded area, don't wait to be told twice.

04:27.807 --> 04:30.682
Leave by the safe route and tell your supervisor.

04:31.815 --> 04:34.477
Things go wrong at the edges. A delivery driver

04:34.477 --> 04:37.252
arrives. A supervisor is in a hurry.

04:37.252 --> 04:39.727
A friend wants to cut through. The answer is always

04:39.727 --> 04:43.190
the same. Stop them, and call the blaster in charge.

04:43.190 --> 04:45.677
Nobody is waved through because they're only going

04:45.677 --> 04:48.152
to be a minute. And if you realize somebody is

04:48.152 --> 04:51.302
still inside the area, tell the blaster right away,

04:51.302 --> 04:54.440
by radio if you have one, from a safe place.

04:54.440 --> 04:56.240
Don't go in to find them yourself.

04:57.356 --> 04:59.106
What if you're inside the area when the warning

04:59.106 --> 05:02.181
goes off? Stay calm. Call the blaster in charge

05:02.181 --> 05:05.281
on the radio, say who you are and where you are,

05:05.281 --> 05:07.306
and follow what they tell you. They can hold the

05:07.306 --> 05:09.769
shot. That's far safer than guessing which way

05:09.769 --> 05:12.619
to run. If you can't reach anyone, get away from

05:12.619 --> 05:14.969
the loaded holes by the shortest safe route,

05:14.969 --> 05:17.944
and stay away from the face. Then tell your supervisor

05:17.944 --> 05:20.906
what happened, so we can fix whatever let it happen.

05:24.235 --> 05:26.660
The shot going off doesn't end the danger.

05:26.660 --> 05:29.072
The guards stay where they are. The rule says that

05:29.072 --> 05:31.760
work does not resume in the blast area until a

05:31.760 --> 05:34.685
person with the ability and experience has examined

05:34.685 --> 05:38.135
it for hazards, and that includes looking for misfires,

05:38.135 --> 05:40.397
which is the next lesson. Underground,

05:40.397 --> 05:43.397
it also means the air has to be cleared and tested,

05:43.397 --> 05:45.147
which is the lesson after that.

05:46.272 --> 05:48.997
Only the blaster in charge gives the all-clear.

05:48.997 --> 05:50.997
Your site will tell you what it is and how you'll

05:50.997 --> 05:54.497
hear it. Until you hear it, the area is closed.

05:55.639 --> 05:58.201
To sum up. Learn the warning at your site:

05:58.201 --> 06:01.839
siren, strobes, and the blocked entrances underground.

06:01.839 --> 06:04.401
When you hear it, leave the blast area and go where

06:04.401 --> 06:06.864
you were told. If you're a guard, stay on your

06:06.864 --> 06:10.126
post and stop everyone. Stay out until the blaster

06:10.126 --> 06:12.826
gives the all-clear. And if you see a problem,

06:12.826 --> 06:15.676
anywhere, stop and tell the blaster in charge.

06:16.788 --> 06:19.763
Your site's plans always override this video.
